{
  "questions": [
    {
      "source_id": "1",
      "source_raw": "1. Which of the following statements is correct?\nA. We can assume a \"spherical cow\" when analyzing the motion of a cow's leg.\nB. We can safely assume a cow is a point mass when analyzing its position change in the\nscale of a mile over a certain time period.\nC. We can never assume the cow is in a vacuum, even when we need to ignore the effect\nof air resistance.\nD. None of the above is correct.",
      "stem": "Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "We can assume a \"spherical cow\" when analyzing the motion of a cow's leg.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This oversimplifies away the leg’s extended shape and joint rotations, which are crucial for leg motion analysis. The “spherical cow” approximation is more appropriate for gross properties (e.g., center-of-mass motion), not limb kinematics."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "We can safely assume a cow is a point mass when analyzing its position change in the scale of a mile over a certain time period.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "For motion over a mile-scale distance, the cow’s size (on the order of ~1 m) is negligible compared with the distance traveled. In that regime, treating the cow as a point mass is a valid modeling approximation for tracking its position (center-of-mass motion)."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "We can never assume the cow is in a vacuum, even when we need to ignore the effect of air resistance.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "In many problems, “ignore air resistance” is effectively the same as using an idealized vacuum model for the forces. Saying we can “never” do so is an absolute claim and is incorrect for idealized physics modeling."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Option B is a standard valid approximation when the object’s size is much smaller than the length scale of motion. Therefore it is not true that none of the statements are correct."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "unknown",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "everyday_activity",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "2",
      "source_raw": "2. Which of the following equations is correct?\nA. 1 ¢ = 1 $\nB. 1 = 0.01\nC. 1 ¢ = 0.01 $\nD. 100 ¢ = 0.01 $",
      "stem": "Which of the following equations is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "1 \\textcent{} = 1 \\$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses cents and dollars: 1 dollar equals 100 cents, not 1 cent. It is off by a factor of 100."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "1 = 0.01",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is a dimensionless false numerical statement; 1 is not equal to 0.01. It likely reflects confusing a conversion factor with an equality."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "1 \\textcent{} = 0.01 \\$",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "By definition, 1 dollar = 100 cents, so 1 cent = (1/100) dollar. Numerically, (1/100) = 0.01, so 1 ¢ = 0.01 $ is correct."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "100 \\textcent{} = 0.01 \\$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This inverts the relationship: 100 cents equals 1 dollar, not 0.01 dollars. It is off by a factor of 100."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "unknown",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "3",
      "source_raw": "3. Which option has the incorrect unit?\nA. Energy: joule\nB. Velocity: meter/second\nC. Acceleration: meter2/second2\nD. Force: kilogram ∙ meter/second2",
      "stem": "Which option has the incorrect unit?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "Energy: joule",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Joule (J) is the SI unit of energy (equivalently N·m). This matches the correct unit for energy."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "Velocity: $\\mathrm{m/s}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Meters per second (m/s) is the SI unit of velocity. This option is correct."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "Acceleration: $\\mathrm{m^2/s^2}$",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Acceleration is the time-derivative of velocity, so its SI unit is (m/s)/s = m/s². The unit m²/s² corresponds to energy per mass (like specific kinetic energy), not acceleration."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "Force: kilogram ∙ meter/ $\\mathrm{s^2}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "A newton is defined as kg·m/s², which is the SI unit of force. This option matches that definition."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "unknown",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "4",
      "source_raw": "4. The raven is sent from the Castle Black, which is at the origin, observing the white\nwalkers at the coordinates 𝑟⃗ = 3𝑖̂+6𝑗̂ at the beginning of season 4, where length units\n4\nare in kilofurlongs. Which of the following statements is correct?\nA. The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 9 kilofurlongs.\nB. The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 6 kilofurlongs.\nC. The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 3 kilofurlongs.\nD. None of the above is correct.",
      "stem": "The raven is sent from the Castle Black, which is at the origin, observing the white walkers at the coordinates $\\vec{r}$ = 3$\\hat{\\imath}$+6$\\hat{\\jmath}$ at the beginning of season 4, where length units are in kilofurlongs. Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 9 kilofurlongs.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This treats distance as the sum of components (3+6), which is not how Euclidean distance works. The correct distance uses the Pythagorean theorem."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 6 kilofurlongs.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses the y-component (6) with the total distance from the origin. The full displacement has both x and y components."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The distance between the white walkers and the Castle Black is 3 kilofurlongs.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses the x-component (3) with the total distance from the origin. The distance must account for both components."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The distance from the origin to (3,6) is $\\sqrt{3^2+6^2}=\\sqrt{45}\\approx 6.71$ kilofurlongs. Since 6.71 is not equal to 9, 6, or 3, none of A–C is correct."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "unknown",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "everyday_activity",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "5",
      "source_raw": "5. If the displacement of an object is given by the function 𝑠⃗ = ((1.3𝑚/𝑠3)𝑡3 +\n(2.7𝑚/𝑠2)𝑡2)𝑖̂, which of the following statement is correct?\nA. This is a constant acceleration motion.\nB. The magnitude of the acceleration is 2.7 m/s2\nC. The magnitude of the acceleration at t = 0 is 0 m/s2\nD. The magnitude of the acceleration at t = 0 is 5.4 m/s2",
      "stem": "If the displacement of an object is given by the function $\\vec{s}$ = ((1.3$\\mathrm{m/s^3}$)t3 + (2.7$\\mathrm{m/s^2}$)t2)$\\hat{\\imath}$, which of the following statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "This is a constant acceleration motion.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Because $s(t)$ contains a $t^3$ term, differentiating twice produces an acceleration that depends on time. Constant acceleration would require $s(t)$ to be at most quadratic in time."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration is 2.7 $\\mathrm{m/s^2}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "2.7 $\\mathrm{m/s^2}$ is a coefficient in the displacement expression, not the acceleration itself. The acceleration is found by taking the second derivative of displacement with respect to time."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at t = 0 is 0 $\\mathrm{m/s^2}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Computing $a(t)=d^2s/dt^2$ gives a nonzero constant term at $t=0$. The mistake is assuming the acceleration must vanish because $t=0$."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at t = 0 is 5.4 $\\mathrm{m/s^2}$",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Differentiate: $v(t)=ds/dt=(3\\cdot 1.3)t^2+(2\\cdot 2.7)t=3.9t^2+5.4t$. Then $a(t)=dv/dt=7.8t+5.4$, so at $t=0$ the acceleration magnitude is $5.4\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "unknown",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "6",
      "source_raw": "6. An object takes 3 second to move from position A (𝑟⃗ = −4.0𝑖̂+10𝑗̂) to position B\n𝐴\n(𝑟⃗ = 2.0𝑖̂+5.0𝑗̂) along a straight path, where length units are in meters. Which of the\n𝐵\nfollowing statements is correct?\nA. The moving distance is 7.8 m.\nB. The speed at t = 0 must be 2.6 m/s.\nC. The acceleration at t = 0 must be 0.87 m/s2\nD. None of the above is correct.",
      "stem": "An object takes 3 second to move from position A ($\\vec{r}$ = −4.0$\\hat{\\imath}$+10$\\hat{\\jmath}$) to position B ($\\vec{r}$ = 2.0$\\hat{\\imath}$+5.0$\\hat{\\jmath}$) along a straight path, where length units are in meters. Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The moving distance is 7.8 m.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Because the motion is along a straight path from A to B, the distance traveled equals the magnitude of the displacement vector. $\\Delta \\vec r=(2-(-4))\\hat\\imath+(5-10)\\hat\\jmath=6\\hat\\imath-5\\hat\\jmath$, so $|\\Delta \\vec r|=\\sqrt{6^2+(-5)^2}=\\sqrt{61}\\approx 7.81\\ \\mathrm{m}$, which rounds to 7.8 m."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The speed at t = 0 must be 2.6 $\\mathrm{m/s}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "2.6 m/s is the average speed only if the speed were constant, which is not stated. Knowing only start/end positions and total time does not uniquely determine the initial speed."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The acceleration at t = 0 must be 0.87 $\\mathrm{m/s^2}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "The problem provides no information about how velocity changes with time, so the acceleration cannot be determined. Many different accelerations could produce the same start/end positions in 3 s."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Statement A is correct because the straight-line distance between the two points is about 7.81 m. Therefore it is not true that none of the statements are correct."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "unknown",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "7",
      "source_raw": "7. A Chinook salmon can jump out of water with a speed v and an angle 𝜃 above the\n0\nhorizontal. What is the speed of the fish when it is at the maximum height?\nA. 𝑣 .\n0\nB. 𝑣 cos𝜃.\n0\nC. 𝑣 sin𝜃.\n0\nD. 0.",
      "stem": "A Chinook salmon can jump out of water with a speed v and an angle $\\theta$ above the horizontal. What is the speed of the fish when it is at the maximum height?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$v_0$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "At maximum height the vertical component of velocity is zero, so the speed is less than the launch speed unless $\\theta=0$. This option ignores the loss of vertical speed due to gravity."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$v_0$ cos$\\theta$.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "In projectile motion without air resistance, the horizontal velocity component stays constant at $v_x=v_0\\cos\\theta$. At the maximum height, $v_y=0$, so the speed equals the magnitude of the horizontal component: $v=v_0\\cos\\theta$."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$v_0$ sin$\\theta$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "$v_0\\sin\\theta$ is the initial vertical component, but at maximum height the vertical component becomes zero. This option confuses the initial vertical speed with the speed at the top."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "0.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Only the vertical component becomes zero at the top; the horizontal component generally remains nonzero. This option incorrectly assumes the projectile momentarily stops completely."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "unknown",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "life_science",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "8",
      "source_raw": "8. A spaceship is traveling with an initial velocity of 𝑣⃗ = (20.1 𝑚/𝑠)𝑖 ̂when its rockets\n0\nfire, imparting an acceleration of 𝑎⃗ = (2.02𝑚⁄𝑠2)𝑖̂+(4.03𝑚⁄𝑠2)𝑗.̂ Based on this\nscenario, which of the following statements is correct?\nA. The x-component of the spaceship’s velocity remains constant.\nB. The y-component of the spaceship’s velocity remains constant.\nC. Both the direction of motion and speed change.\nD. None of the above is correct.",
      "stem": "A spaceship is traveling with an initial velocity of $\\vec{v}$ = (20.1 $\\mathrm{m/s}$)$\\hat{\\imath}$ when its rockets fire, imparting an acceleration of $\\vec{a}$ = (2.02$\\mathrm{m/s^2}$)$\\hat{\\imath}$+(4.03$\\mathrm{m/s^2}$)$\\hat{\\jmath}$. Based on this scenario, which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The x-component of the spaceship’s velocity remains constant.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Since $a_x=2.02\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}\\neq 0$, the x-component of velocity must change with time. A constant x-velocity would require $a_x=0$."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The y-component of the spaceship’s velocity remains constant.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Since $a_y=4.03\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}\\neq 0$, the y-component of velocity changes with time. It starts at 0 but then increases due to the positive y-acceleration."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "Both the direction of motion and speed change.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Because the acceleration has both x and y components, the velocity gains a y-component and the direction of motion changes from purely +x to a tilted direction. Also, with nonzero acceleration components, the magnitude of the velocity (speed) changes over time as the velocity vector changes."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Option C correctly describes the effects of a 2D acceleration on the velocity vector. Therefore it is not true that none of the statements are correct."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "unknown",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "9",
      "source_raw": "9. If the magnitudes of both charges are tripled and their separation is also tripled, what\nhappens to the magnitude of the electrostatic force that each charge exerts on the other?\nA. It stays the same.\nB. It decreases by a factor of 31/2.\nC. It increases by a factor of 3.\nD. It increases by a factor of 9.\nE. It increases by a factor of 31/2.",
      "stem": "If the magnitudes of both charges are tripled and their separation is also tripled, what happens to the magnitude of the electrostatic force that each charge exerts on the other?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "It stays the same.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "By Coulomb’s law, $F=k\\frac{|q_1q_2|}{r^2}$. Tripling both charges multiplies the numerator by $3\\cdot 3=9$, while tripling the separation multiplies $r^2$ by $3^2=9$, so the factors cancel and the force is unchanged."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "It decreases by a factor of 31/2.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is a common scaling mistake when applying powers to the distance dependence. The inverse-square effect from $r^2$ exactly cancels the factor from tripling both charges here."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "It increases by a factor of 3.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This ignores that both charges change (giving a factor of 9) and that distance changes affect the force via an inverse square. The net result is not a factor of 3."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "It increases by a factor of 9.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This accounts for tripling both charges (factor 9) but neglects the $1/r^2$ decrease from tripling the separation (factor 1/9). Including both changes yields no net change."
        },
        {
          "key": "E",
          "text": "It increases by a factor of 31/2.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This reflects an incorrect combination of linear and square-root scaling. Coulomb’s law has a $1/r^2$ dependence, not a square-root dependence."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "unknown",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "10",
      "source_raw": "10. An object can escape a planet with radius R and mass M at an escape speed given by 𝑣 =\n√2𝐺𝑀⁄𝑅, where 𝐺 is the universal gravitational constant. Based on this expression for\nescape speed, which of the following statements is correct?\nA. A heavier object requires a higher speed to escape from the planet.\nB. A lighter object requires a lower speed to escape from the planet.\nC. An object requires a higher speed to escape from a planet with smaller mass and a\nlarger radius.\nD. An object requires a higher speed to escape from a planet with greater mass and a\nsmaller radius.",
      "stem": "An object can escape a planet with radius R and mass M at an escape speed given by $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2GM}{R}}$, where G is the universal gravitational constant. Based on this expression for escape speed, which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "A heavier object requires a higher speed to escape from the planet.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "The escape-speed formula $v=\\sqrt{2GM/R}$ does not depend on the escaping object’s mass. This option incorrectly assumes heavier objects need higher escape speed."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "A lighter object requires a lower speed to escape from the planet.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Escape speed is independent of the object’s mass in Newtonian gravity (neglecting atmosphere/drag). This option confuses energy required with speed required."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "An object requires a higher speed to escape from a planet with smaller mass and a larger radius.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "From $v\\propto\\sqrt{M/R}$, smaller $M$ and larger $R$ both reduce escape speed. This option reverses the dependence on $M$ and $R$."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "An object requires a higher speed to escape from a planet with greater mass and a smaller radius.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The escape speed scales as $v\\propto\\sqrt{M/R}$. Increasing the planet’s mass increases $v$, and decreasing the radius also increases $v$, so a more massive and more compact planet requires a higher escape speed."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "unknown",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "11",
      "source_raw": "11. The force 𝐹⃗ = (3.04N)𝑖̂+(4.01N)𝑗̂. acts on a particle as it moves along the x axis from\nx = 0.33 m to x = 5.03 m. What is the work, in joules, done by the force on the particle?\nA. 20.5 J\nB. 14.3 J\nC. 5.00 J\nD. 35.7 J",
      "stem": "The force $\\vec{F}$ = (3.04$\\mathrm{N}$)$\\hat{\\imath}$+(4.01$\\mathrm{N}$)$\\hat{\\jmath}$. acts on a particle as it moves along the x axis from x = 0.33 m to x = 5.03 m. What is the work, in joules, done by the force on the particle?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "20.5 J",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This likely (incorrectly) uses the magnitude of the force instead of the component parallel to the displacement. Work is the dot product $\\vec F\\cdot\\Delta\\vec r$, not $|\\vec F|\\,|\\Delta\\vec r|$ unless they are parallel."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "14.3 J",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The displacement is along the x-axis only: $\\Delta\\vec r=(5.03-0.33)\\hat\\imath=4.70\\hat\\imath\\ \\mathrm{m}$. Thus $W=\\vec F\\cdot\\Delta\\vec r=F_x\\Delta x=(3.04\\ \\mathrm{N})(4.70\\ \\mathrm{m})=14.288\\ \\mathrm{J}\\approx 14.3\\ \\mathrm{J}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "5.00 J",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This may come from using only a small part of the displacement or rounding incorrectly. The correct dot-product calculation gives about 14.3 J."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "35.7 J",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This resembles multiplying the force magnitude by distance, which overcounts because the y-component does no work when motion is purely along x. Only $F_x$ contributes here."
        }
      ],
      "has_explicit_answer": false,
      "difficulty": "unknown",
      "question_type": "multiple_choice",
      "theme": "physics_abstract",
      "topic_tags": []
    },
    {
      "source_id": "12",
      "source_raw": "12. A 4.5 kg cart initially moving at 2.1 m/s collides with a wall and rebounds at a speed of\n1.3 m/s moving backward. The collision lasts for 0.2 seconds. What is the average force\nexerted on the cart during the collision?\nA. 47.3 N.\nB. 18 N.\nC. 76.5 N.\nD. None of the above is correct.",
      "stem": "A 4.5 $\\mathrm{kg}$ cart initially moving at 2.1 $\\mathrm{m/s}$ collides with a wall and rebounds at a speed of 1.3 $\\mathrm{m/s}$ moving backward. The collision lasts for 0.2 seconds. What is the average force exerted on the cart during the collision?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "47.3 $\\mathrm{N}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This likely uses $\\Delta v = 2.1-1.3=0.8\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$, incorrectly treating the rebound speed as still positive. The velocity changes sign when the cart rebounds."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "18 $\\mathrm{N}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is too small and may come from dividing by an incorrect time or using only one of the speeds. The correct impulse calculation yields a larger magnitude."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "76.5 $\\mathrm{N}$.",
          "is_correct": true,
